150. Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation
Problem

You are given an array of strings tokens representing an arithmetic expression in Reverse Polish Notation (Postfix Expression).

Evaluate the expression and return the result.

Valid operators are:

+, -, *, /

Division should truncate toward zero.

Example 1
Input:
tokens = ["2","1","+","3","*"]

Output:
9

Explanation

((2 + 1) * 3)

= 9
Example 2
Input:
tokens = ["4","13","5","/","+"]

Output:
6

Explanation

13 / 5 = 2

4 + 2 = 6
Example 3
Input:

["10","6","9","3","+","-11","*","/","*","17","+","5","+"]

Output:

22
What is Reverse Polish Notation?

Instead of writing

2 + 3

RPN writes

2 3 +

Another example

(2 + 1) × 3

becomes

2 1 + 3 *
Key Observation

Whenever an operator appears,

it always works on the last two numbers.

This follows LIFO (Last In First Out).

Hence Stack is the ideal data structure.

Possible approaches:

Brute Force
Better (Recursive Evaluation)
Stack (Most Optimal)

Approach 1: Brute Force
Intuition

Repeatedly scan the array.

Whenever

Number Number Operator

is found,

evaluate it,

replace the three tokens with the answer,

and continue until only one element remains.

Java
class Solution {

    public int evalRPN(String[] tokens) {

        List<String> list = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(tokens));

        while (list.size() > 1) {

            for (int i = 2; i < list.size(); i++) {

                String op = list.get(i);

                if (op.equals("+") || op.equals("-")
                        || op.equals("*") || op.equals("/")) {

                    int a = Integer.parseInt(list.get(i - 2));
                    int b = Integer.parseInt(list.get(i - 1));

                    int ans = 0;

                    if (op.equals("+")) ans = a + b;
                    else if (op.equals("-")) ans = a - b;
                    else if (op.equals("*")) ans = a * b;
                    else ans = a / b;

                    list.subList(i - 2, i + 1).clear();
                    list.add(i - 2, String.valueOf(ans));

                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        return Integer.parseInt(list.get(0));
    }
}

