Approach 2: Better (Recursive Evaluation)
Intuition

Read the expression from right to left.

If current token is a number → return it.
If current token is an operator → recursively evaluate the next two operands.

Java
class Solution {

    int index;

    public int evalRPN(String[] tokens) {

        index = tokens.length - 1;

        return solve(tokens);
    }

    private int solve(String[] tokens) {

        String s = tokens[index--];

        if (!(s.equals("+") || s.equals("-") ||
              s.equals("*") || s.equals("/"))) {

            return Integer.parseInt(s);
        }

        int right = solve(tokens);
        int left = solve(tokens);

        switch (s) {

            case "+": return left + right;
            case "-": return left - right;
            case "*": return left * right;
            default: return left / right;
        }
    }
}

Complexity
Time : O(n)
Space : O(n) (Recursion Stack)

Approach 3: Stack ⭐ (Most Optimal)
Intuition

Traverse tokens from left to right.

If token is a number → Push into stack.
If token is an operator:
Pop second operand (b)
Pop first operand (a)
Compute a op b
Push result back

Finally,

the remaining element is the answer.

Algorithm
